{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your host, proxy, or React Native setup buffers or breaks streamed responses. Or you want one plain response per run. Send the run as one JSON body. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" works the same: messages, tool calls, approvals, and errors."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"1-return-json-from-the-server","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Return JSON from the server"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run the chat to its end with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream: false"},{"type":"text","value":", then send the result with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toJsonResponse()"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toJsonResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n    stream: false,\n  });\n  console.log(result.text);\n  return toJsonResponse(result);\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result.text"},{"type":"text","value":" is the full reply. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result.chunks"},{"type":"text","value":" holds every chunk of the run, and the client reads them from the response."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also pass the stream from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":". Then pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"request.signal"},{"type":"text","value":", so that the run stops when the client leaves:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toJsonResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  });\n  return toJsonResponse(stream, { signal: request.signal });\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The body is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ chunks, offset?, done }"},{"type":"text","value":". Without durability, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"done"},{"type":"text","value":" is always "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", and the body holds the whole run."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"2-connect-from-the-client","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. Connect from the client"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchJson()"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, fetchJson } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, isLoading } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchJson(\"/api/chat\"),\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((message) => (\n        <p key={message.id}>\n          {message.parts.map((part) => (part.type === \"text\" ? part.content : \"\"))}\n        </p>\n      ))}\n      <button disabled={isLoading} onClick={() => void sendMessage(\"Hello\")}>\n        Send\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchJson"},{"type":"text","value":" sends the same POST body as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchHttpStream"},{"type":"text","value":". It takes the same options: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"headers"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"body"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"credentials"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchClient"},{"type":"text","value":", and dynamic functions. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./request-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request Options"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"long-runs-and-reloads","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Long runs and reloads"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A long agent run can take more time than your host lets one request live. Add durability, and the server replies before the timeout. The client then asks for the rest."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a durability adapter, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"request.signal"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxWaitMs"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toJsonResponse"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" handler with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeJsonResponse"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  resumeJsonResponse,\n  toJsonResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({ adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"), messages, threadId, runId });\n  return toJsonResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n    signal: request.signal,\n    maxWaitMs: 10_000,\n  });\n}\n\n// fetchJson polls GET ?runId=...&offset=... until the reply has done: true.\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  return resumeJsonResponse({ adapter: memoryStream(request) });\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What happens:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxWaitMs"}]},{"type":"text","value":": after 10 seconds, the server replies with the chunks it has and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"done: false"},{"type":"text","value":". The run keeps going and fills the log."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Polling"}]},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchJson"},{"type":"text","value":" waits "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pollIntervalMs"},{"type":"text","value":", then sends "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET ?runId=<id>&offset=<offset>"},{"type":"text","value":". Each reply holds only new chunks. It stops at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"done: true"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"signal"}]},{"type":"text","value":": when the client leaves, the run does not stop. It keeps going in the log."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reloads"}]},{"type":"text","value":": after a reload, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":" sends "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET ?runId=<id>&offset=-1"},{"type":"text","value":" to the same handler and reads the run from the start."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeJsonResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" waits at most 1000 ms for new chunks before it replies. It never calls the model."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client from step 2 needs no change. To set the wait between polls (default 250 ms), pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pollIntervalMs"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchJson } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\nconst connection = fetchJson(\"/api/chat\", { pollIntervalMs: 500 });","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass this "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connection"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream(request)"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps the log in one process. If your requests run on many processes, use a production adapter. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../resumable-streams/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resumable Streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limits"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No live text."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The answer appears when a reply arrives. With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxWaitMs"},{"type":"text","value":", it appears in parts, one part per reply."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The run must outlive the reply."}]},{"type":"text","value":" After an early reply, the run continues in the same process. Your host must keep that work alive after the response goes out."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first POST is not retried."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A retry can start the run two times. A failed poll is retried with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconnect"},{"type":"text","value":" option. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../resumable-streams/advanced#reconnection-bounding","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconnection bounding"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchJson"},{"type":"text","value":", not "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetcher"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" fetcher that returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response"},{"type":"text","value":" is parsed as SSE."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Send a message. When the run ends, the full reply appears in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":", with no stream between your server and the browser."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"1-return-json-from-the-server","text":"1. Return JSON from the server","level":2},{"id":"2-connect-from-the-client","text":"2. Connect from the client","level":2},{"id":"long-runs-and-reloads","text":"Long runs and reloads","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYour host, proxy, or React Native setup buffers or breaks streamed responses. Or you want one plain response per run. Send the run as one JSON body. `useChat` works the same: messages, tool calls, approvals, and errors.\n\n## 1. Return JSON from the server\n\nRun the chat to its end with `stream: false`, then send the result with `toJsonResponse()`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toJsonResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n    stream: false,\n  });\n  console.log(result.text);\n  return toJsonResponse(result);\n}\n```\n\n`result.text` is the full reply. `result.chunks` holds every chunk of the run, and the client reads them from the response.\n\nYou can also pass the stream from `chat()`. Then pass `request.signal`, so that the run stops when the client leaves:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toJsonResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  });\n  return toJsonResponse(stream, { signal: request.signal });\n}\n```\n\nThe body is `{ chunks, offset?, done }`. Without durability, `done` is always `true`, and the body holds the whole run.\n\n## 2. Connect from the client\n\nPass `fetchJson()` to `useChat`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, fetchJson } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, isLoading } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchJson(\"/api/chat\"),\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((message) => (\n        <p key={message.id}>\n          {message.parts.map((part) => (part.type === \"text\" ? part.content : \"\"))}\n        </p>\n      ))}\n      <button disabled={isLoading} onClick={() => void sendMessage(\"Hello\")}>\n        Send\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n`fetchJson` sends the same POST body as `fetchHttpStream`. It takes the same options: `headers`, `body`, `credentials`, `fetchClient`, and dynamic functions. See [Request Options](./request-options).\n\n## Long runs and reloads\n\nA long agent run can take more time than your host lets one request live. Add durability, and the server replies before the timeout. The client then asks for the rest.\n\n1. Pass a durability adapter, `request.signal`, and `maxWaitMs` to `toJsonResponse`.\n2. Add a `GET` handler with `resumeJsonResponse`.\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  resumeJsonResponse,\n  toJsonResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({ adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"), messages, threadId, runId });\n  return toJsonResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n    signal: request.signal,\n    maxWaitMs: 10_000,\n  });\n}\n\n// fetchJson polls GET ?runId=...&offset=... until the reply has done: true.\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  return resumeJsonResponse({ adapter: memoryStream(request) });\n}\n```\n\nWhat happens:\n\n- **`maxWaitMs`**: after 10 seconds, the server replies with the chunks it has and `done: false`. The run keeps going and fills the log.\n- **Polling**: `fetchJson` waits `pollIntervalMs`, then sends `GET ?runId=<id>&offset=<offset>`. Each reply holds only new chunks. It stops at `done: true`.\n- **`signal`**: when the client leaves, the run does not stop. It keeps going in the log.\n- **Reloads**: after a reload, `joinRun` sends `GET ?runId=<id>&offset=-1` to the same handler and reads the run from the start.\n\n`resumeJsonResponse` waits at most 1000 ms for new chunks before it replies. It never calls the model.\n\nThe client from step 2 needs no change. To set the wait between polls (default 250 ms), pass `pollIntervalMs`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { fetchJson } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\nconst connection = fetchJson(\"/api/chat\", { pollIntervalMs: 500 });\n```\n\nPass this `connection` to `useChat`.\n\n`memoryStream(request)` keeps the log in one process. If your requests run on many processes, use a production adapter. See [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview).\n\n## Limits\n\n- **No live text.** The answer appears when a reply arrives. With `maxWaitMs`, it appears in parts, one part per reply.\n- **The run must outlive the reply.** After an early reply, the run continues in the same process. Your host must keep that work alive after the response goes out.\n- **The first POST is not retried.** A retry can start the run two times. A failed poll is retried with the `reconnect` option. See [Reconnection bounding](../resumable-streams/advanced#reconnection-bounding).\n- **Use `fetchJson`, not `fetcher`.** A `useChat` fetcher that returns a `Response` is parsed as SSE.\n\nSend a message. When the run ends, the full reply appears in `messages`, with no stream between your server and the browser.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: JSON\nid: transports-json\norder: 9\ndescription: \"Send each chat run as one plain JSON response with fetchJson and toJsonResponse, for hosts and proxies that buffer or break streams, and poll long runs with resumeJsonResponse.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - json transport\n  - non-streaming chat\n  - fetchJson\n  - toJsonResponse\n  - resumeJsonResponse\n  - polling\n  - serverless timeout\n---\n\nYour host, proxy, or React Native setup buffers or breaks streamed responses. Or you want one plain response per run. Send the run as one JSON body. `useChat` works the same: messages, tool calls, approvals, and errors.\n\n## 1. Return JSON from the server\n\nRun the chat to its end with `stream: false`, then send the result with `toJsonResponse()`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toJsonResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n    stream: false,\n  });\n  console.log(result.text);\n  return toJsonResponse(result);\n}\n```\n\n`result.text` is the full reply. `result.chunks` holds every chunk of the run, and the client reads them from the response.\n\nYou can also pass the stream from `chat()`. Then pass `request.signal`, so that the run stops when the client leaves:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toJsonResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  });\n  return toJsonResponse(stream, { signal: request.signal });\n}\n```\n\nThe body is `{ chunks, offset?, done }`. Without durability, `done` is always `true`, and the body holds the whole run.\n\n## 2. Connect from the client\n\nPass `fetchJson()` to `useChat`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, fetchJson } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, isLoading } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchJson(\"/api/chat\"),\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((message) => (\n        <p key={message.id}>\n          {message.parts.map((part) => (part.type === \"text\" ? part.content : \"\"))}\n        </p>\n      ))}\n      <button disabled={isLoading} onClick={() => void sendMessage(\"Hello\")}>\n        Send\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n`fetchJson` sends the same POST body as `fetchHttpStream`. It takes the same options: `headers`, `body`, `credentials`, `fetchClient`, and dynamic functions. See [Request Options](./request-options).\n\n## Long runs and reloads\n\nA long agent run can take more time than your host lets one request live. Add durability, and the server replies before the timeout. The client then asks for the rest.\n\n1. Pass a durability adapter, `request.signal`, and `maxWaitMs` to `toJsonResponse`.\n2. Add a `GET` handler with `resumeJsonResponse`.\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  resumeJsonResponse,\n  toJsonResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({ adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"), messages, threadId, runId });\n  return toJsonResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n    signal: request.signal,\n    maxWaitMs: 10_000,\n  });\n}\n\n// fetchJson polls GET ?runId=...&offset=... until the reply has done: true.\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  return resumeJsonResponse({ adapter: memoryStream(request) });\n}\n```\n\nWhat happens:\n\n- **`maxWaitMs`**: after 10 seconds, the server replies with the chunks it has and `done: false`. The run keeps going and fills the log.\n- **Polling**: `fetchJson` waits `pollIntervalMs`, then sends `GET ?runId=<id>&offset=<offset>`. Each reply holds only new chunks. It stops at `done: true`.\n- **`signal`**: when the client leaves, the run does not stop. It keeps going in the log.\n- **Reloads**: after a reload, `joinRun` sends `GET ?runId=<id>&offset=-1` to the same handler and reads the run from the start.\n\n`resumeJsonResponse` waits at most 1000 ms for new chunks before it replies. It never calls the model.\n\nThe client from step 2 needs no change. To set the wait between polls (default 250 ms), pass `pollIntervalMs`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { fetchJson } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\nconst connection = fetchJson(\"/api/chat\", { pollIntervalMs: 500 });\n```\n\nPass this `connection` to `useChat`.\n\n`memoryStream(request)` keeps the log in one process. If your requests run on many processes, use a production adapter. See [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview).\n\n## Limits\n\n- **No live text.** The answer appears when a reply arrives. With `maxWaitMs`, it appears in parts, one part per reply.\n- **The run must outlive the reply.** After an early reply, the run continues in the same process. Your host must keep that work alive after the response goes out.\n- **The first POST is not retried.** A retry can start the run two times. A failed poll is retried with the `reconnect` option. See [Reconnection bounding](../resumable-streams/advanced#reconnection-bounding).\n- **Use `fetchJson`, not `fetcher`.** A `useChat` fetcher that returns a `Response` is parsed as SSE.\n\nSend a message. When the run ends, the full reply appears in `messages`, with no stream between your server and the browser.\n"}
